Problem Before Java 25

In earlier Java versions, a constructor call to this(...) or super(...) had to be the very first statement in the constructor.

For example, this was illegal:

class Employee {
    Employee(String name) {
        System.out.println("Validating...");
        super();  // Compile Error
    }
}

You would get:

Constructor call must be the first statement in a constructor
Java 25: Flexible Constructor Bodies

Java 25 allows certain statements to appear before the constructor delegation call.

Example:

class Employee {

    Employee(String name) {
        name = name.trim();     // Allowed before this(...)
        this(name, 0);
    }

    Employee(String name, int age) {
        System.out.println(name + " " + age);
    }
}

Before Java 25, the above code was not allowed because this(name, 0) was not the first statement.

Why was this added?

Developers often need to:

Validate arguments
Normalize data
Perform simple computations

before calling another constructor.

Previously, they had to create helper methods:

class Employee {

    Employee(String name) {
        this(normalize(name), 0);
    }

    static String normalize(String name) {
        return name.trim();
    }

    Employee(String name, int age) {
    }
}

Java 25 removes much of this boilerplate.

What is allowed before this() or super()?

Typical examples:

name = name.trim();
age = Math.max(age, 0);
var normalized = normalize(name);

The compiler ensures that no illegal access to the not-yet-constructed object occurs.

What is still NOT allowed?

You cannot use this before the constructor delegation completes.

Example:

class Employee {

    Employee(String name) {
        System.out.println(this); // Not allowed
        this(name, 0);
    }

    Employee(String name, int age) {}
}

Reason:

The object is not fully initialized yet.

